Put ('build:' + (& $slug 'Blues guitarist playing in an empty bar while blue smoke becomes memories of people hes loved')) 'The tattoo: Blues guitarist playing in an empty bar while blue smoke becomes memories of people hes loved. imaginative high-end contemporary music tattoo artwork treating music as fandom, memory, identity, culture, emotion, history, performance and the soundtrack of a human life rather than merely a collection of instruments and musical symbols; freely draw inspiration from musicians, bands, musical eras, concerts, fan culture, songs, albums, listening technology, nightlife, musical genres, instruments, family memories, relationships and personal life moments; when a famous artist or band is explicitly named in the individual concept, the artist or band name may appear clearly and intentionally as part of an original fan-homage tattoo composition, but create new imagery and composition rather than reproducing an existing album cover, photograph, logo or merchandise design; freely range between rock and roll, pop, punk, metal, hip-hop, rap, country, blues, jazz, soul, funk, disco, electronic, dance, classical, Latin, reggae and other musical worlds according to the concept; freely vary between American Traditional, Neo-Traditional, realism, black and grey, illustrative tattoo art, psychedelic, Gothic, Western, graffiti-influenced, Chicano-influenced, Y2K, fine line, blackwork, surrealism, ornamental, colorful contemporary tattooing and completely unexpected visual languages; show the EXPERIENCE of music through movement, crowds, stage light, rhythm, bass, atmosphere, emotion, memory and visual transformation; instruments and music technology may appear but should become part of a larger idea rather than generic isolated clip art; avoid repetitive guitars, treble clefs, music notes, roses wrapped around microphones, generic headphones and generic vinyl-record compositions; use text only when the individual concept specifically calls for an artist name, band name, date, personal label or other meaningful lettering; THE ART CAN BE IMAGINATIVE BUT SPECIFIC MUSIC-RELATED FACTS PROVIDED BY THE USER MUST REMAIN ACCURATE, preserve supplied artist names, dates, concert details, instrument details, personal handwriting, notation and other meaningful source material rather than inventing or altering it; make each design feel like MUSIC MATTERS TO THIS PERSON. In the right panel it is on the outside of her upper arm, wearing a sleeveless top'
